<h2>Project Status</h2>
<p>This tool is currently in its infancy and it may be buggy or incorrect while I update the information. It is under active development as of September 2026. You can view the progress on <a href="https://github.com/petermodracee/ca-sea-change-atlas" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.</p>

<h2>About the California Sea Change Atlas</h2>
<p>The California Sea Change Atlas (CASCA) was originally envisioned as a recreation of the "Sea the Future" tool summarizing sea level rise and flood visualization tools and an effort to build out a mapping component which did not exist in the original tool, enabling side-by-side view of the different layers from the different source models. However, climate science evolves constantly and research and funding is cyclical; some of the 12 tools and models from the original tool have not been updated or maintained, and new ones have cropped up in their place. This is intended as a spiritual successor.</p>

<p>The CASCA <a href="{{ '/compare/' | url }}">comparison tool</a> is intended as a spiritual successor of the "Sea the Future" tool describing the differences in each model and resource. As with the original, this is not intended to tell you which tool is ultimately the "best" or "worst"; it's only intended to help with determining which tool may be most useful for your given question or project. In addition, the original tool did not have a map that allows the user to flip back and forth between resources for comparison, requiring pulling up the multiple resources side by side. For how the sources themselves were chosen, see <a href="{{ '/compare/about/' | url }}">About the sources</a>.</p>

<p>The CASCA <a href="{{ '/map/' | url }}">map</a> is an attempt to collate any resources that provide publically accessible data, primarily from government or openly licensable sources (e.g. in the public domain), onto a single map. As a Californian, I wanted to keep the tool California-centric, both because I call it home and because the data is a little more familiar to me to verify. In an effort to maintain it's longevity, even if I've moved on to other projects, I have hosted this on GitHub with GNU licensing so others may continue the work if desired.</p>

<p>CASCA is built on <a href="https://www.11ty.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">eleventy</a> and hosted on <a href="https://pages.github.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Pages</a>. The map is powered by <a href="https://leafletjs.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Leaflet</a> and uses <a href="https://github.com/plotly/datasets" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Plotly datasets</a> for region boundaries (e.g. county). <a href="http://claude.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude</a> helped out with the code, but the web copy text and eventually research into the different tools are all my own.</p>

<h2>A brief history of the "Sea the Future" Tool</h2>
<p><a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20211230220741/https://www.seathefuture.org/#/filter" target="_blank" rel="noopener">"Sea the Future"</a>, originally hosted at seathefuture.org (no longer functional), was a decision-support tool built by the California State Coastal Conservancy and NOAA's Office for Coastal Management to help planners compare 12 sea-level-rise and coastal-flooding visualization tools covering California's coastline. According to the <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5vEpFthI0sM" target="_blank" rel="noopener">intro and demo video</a> from the <a href="https://scc.ca.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">California State Coastal Conservancy</a>, "Sea the Future" was born from an exercise called "Lifting the Fog" to collaborate on navigating the different resources available to describe sea level rise tools, ultimately culminating in a <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20260129050531/https://sealevel.climatecentral.org/matrix/CA.html?v=1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tool comparison matrix</a> hosted by <a href="https://www.climatecentral.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Climate Central</a>, and inspired by <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20260615164133/http://gulftree.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gulf TREE</a>, a decsion-support tool for climate change related resources in the Northern Gulf of Mexico.</p>

<p>Unfortunately, tools like these most likely require a significant effort to maintain and update, and projects like these are often supported through grants which are not guaranteed and are not perpetual, especially if certain government administrations deprioritize funding and support for <a href="https://www.americanprogress.org/article/how-trumps-cuts-to-fema-and-nws-are-leaving-communities-defenseless-against-extreme-weather-disasters/" target="_blank">climate science agencies and programs</a>. Although no documentation exists as to why it shut down, I presume the high cost of maintaining these resources, whether funding or effort, ran out; servers were shut down, and resources were quietly sunsetted. As a result, all of the tools here have suffered from <a href="https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1014627511641" target="_blank" rel="noopener">linkrot</a>. I have attempted to reconstruct as much as I could through the use of archive links via the WaybackMachine; the last useful snapshot of each URL is included in this section.</p>

<p>A text description of what "Sea the Future" was, and a starting point for this project, is hosted at the <a href="https://www.adaptationclearinghouse.org/resources/california-e-sea-the-future-e-tool.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Georgetown Climate Center's Adaptation Clearinghouse</a>. As a note, some of the requests from the original video were for "mapping tools side by side" and an address lookup; the CASCA Map component was intended to respond to these feature requests and is not a part of the original tool.</p>

<h2>About Me</h2>
<p>My name is Peter Modracee and I am a graduate student at the <a href="https://www.sandiego.edu/engineering/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">University of San Diego Shiley-Marcos School of Engineering</a> pursuing a <a href="https://onlinedegrees.sandiego.edu/masters-engineering-sustainability-health/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Masters of Science in Engineering, Sustainability, and Health</a>. This project was originally inspired by a module of the Sustainable Water course (ESH-511) examining community strategies and tools to address risks stemming from sea level changes and flood risk as a result of climate change. "Sea the Change" was one of the referenced tools but was no longer available; this side project is a result of trying to find out what the original tool was and what purpose it served. This is an attempt to solve a technical problem of parsing different resources, but does not solve the adaptive challenges of creating strategies in response to climate change or sea level rise.</p>

<p>I welcome questions, concerns, suggestions, and feedback on the <a href="https://github.com/petermodracee/ca-sea-change-atlas/discussions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">repo discussions page at GitHub</a>. You may also contact me directly via <a href="https://github.com/petermodracee" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>, <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/peter-modracee/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LinkedIn</a> or you may e-mail me at first initial last name at sandiego dot edu.</p>

